  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 109 PM EST SUN NOV 15 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, an expansive midlevel ridge\nwill be located over the interior United States. This ridge is \nexpected to amplify and translate eastward through Wednesday. Even\nas a shortwave trough progresses northeast along the periphery of\nthe ridge on Thursday, any impacts will remain far away from \neastern Kentucky; the only expected consequence is a slight \nflattening of the ridge, with 500 mb heights ticking down somewhat\nlocally, although heights will still remain anomalously high for \nthis time of year. This synoptic pattern will keep weather \nuneventful across the forecast area until perhaps the very end of \nthe long term period. Late Sunday, a closed upper level low moving\ninto the Great Lakes could bring our next chance for rain, with \nthe NBM finally introducing mentionable PoPs into the forecast \narea at this time. However, there is still considerable model \ndisagreement regarding the evolution of this feature. \nDeterministic guidance is split between troughing and ridging at \nthis time, with LREF 500 mb heights showing a whopping 12 dam \ndifference between 10th and 90th percentiles in our area on Sunday\nnight (and with considerably higher uncertainty in the Great \nLakes region, where the low would likely track). Therefore, \nconfidence in any rain remains low for now. Instead, the main \nstory of the long term forecast will be an extended period of dry \nand quiet weather.\n\nAt the surface, high pressure will dominate the forecast, with \nthe center of the surface high just slightly to the east of the \nforecast area on Tuesday morning. As the midlevel ridge shifts \neastward, so will the surface high, with light southerly to \nsouthwesterly winds around this feature. Temperatures will trend a\nlittle warmer each day, with highs climbing into the upper 60s \nand perhaps low 70s by the end of the period, substantially above \naverage. Dewpoints will trend upward slightly as well, \nparticularly as flow becomes more southwesterly by the weekend, \nalthough large scale moisture return is not expected. As the ridge\naxis moves east of the forecast area and flow becomes more \nsouthwesterly aloft, increased moisture in the atmospheric column \nwill allow a few clouds to return to the forecast, marking a shift\nfrom \"sunny\" to \"mostly sunny\" skies, but overall moisture will \nstill be meager and below normal into the weekend. With largely \nclear skies and a weak pressure gradient, notable ridge/valley \ntemperature splits should develop each night. Although MOS \nguidance suggests ridges will likely remain above freezing on \nTuesday night, valley locations will drop into the 20s. Looking \nbeyond Tuesday night, lows will not be immune to the \naforementioned warming trend, and minTs will generally be a degree\nor two warmer each night. Beyond these cool overnight \ntemperatures, no impactful weather is expected.",
